  "body": "<p>Trougar wants a fight he can trust and companions he can trust beside him. His disputes with Minerva remain sharp, but on the road he also tries to heal a fallen attacker, follows a clue in the roots and asks why Yen has to keep fighting.</p><h2 id=\"trougar-planning-and-refusal\">A place in the company he can refuse</h2><p>Trougar introduces himself to Scribonia as a holy warrior of Cala. He says he has been lost at sea for months and knows little of the intended ruin. He asks what equipment and preparation are needed, admits that darkness is a problem for him, and offers information from the traders’ ledgers.</p><p>He is willing to set aside his spider mount when Scribonia asks. The harder question is Minerva. Their earlier confrontation still matters to him, and their accounts of it remain opposed. When Scribonia keeps her in the expedition, Trougar says he will not go. He leaves and drops the offered gold before departing. Scribonia does not demand his service.</p><p>That is a refusal of this company under these conditions. It does not establish an oath to avoid Minerva forever, or settle their dispute about the earlier mission.</p><h2 id=\"trougar-child-and-roots\">A clue he follows while still angry</h2><p>In the later Verdelune quest, he travels with Minerva and Thorn anyway. He keeps the rear, watches Minerva closely and questions what she has not told them. When a Child of Viota attacks and falls, he attempts healing. It does not work. He then refuses Minerva’s demand that his spider carry the corpse.</p><p>The tension continues when Minerva stops at a tree and addresses it. Trougar asks whether the detour serves her own aims. He gives Thorn spectacles to help the search, offers his own imperfect interpretations of the riddle, and ultimately follows the old roots farther into the forest.</p><p>He is the one who sees the sword-wielding woman ahead and calls for help. Minerva initially thinks he means to attack her; he explains that she is fighting something else. A sharp exchange becomes a useful correction before they enter the clearing.</p><h2 id=\"trougar-questions-for-yen\">The questions combat does not answer</h2><p>Trougar studies the fight before committing himself. He notices the Children of Viota changing near the stone and asks Yen what would happen if they touched it. Her account of a threatened village leads him to ask why she was banished. He tries another explanation—that this is a protest against the place’s order—and she corrects him: she wants to understand what is already broken.</p><p>He has no ready answer to her questions about lost new life. He turns to Minerva for what she knows and asks Yen how they could help. When Minerva’s mirror shows the First Passage crossing, he asks who the figure is and whether she could be brought back.</p><p>The scene leaves his distrust visible alongside his willingness to look, ask and help. <a href=\"#song-the-wind-cannot-finish\">The full expedition</a> preserves the choices and disagreements that bring the company this far; it does not make him a scholar of the island or reconcile him with every companion.</p>",
